**Question:** Create a small parent/child pair (Wards, Beds) with ON UPDATE CASCADE, change a ward code, and show the beds follow.

```sql
CREATE TABLE healthcare.Wards (WardCode varchar(5) PRIMARY KEY);
CREATE TABLE healthcare.Beds (
    BedID int PRIMARY KEY,
    WardCode varchar(5) NOT NULL
        REFERENCES healthcare.Wards (WardCode) ON UPDATE CASCADE
);
INSERT INTO healthcare.Wards VALUES ('W1');
INSERT INTO healthcare.Beds VALUES (1, 'W1'), (2, 'W1');

UPDATE healthcare.Wards SET WardCode = 'CARD' WHERE WardCode = 'W1';

SELECT BedID, WardCode FROM healthcare.Beds;
```

**How it works:** ON UPDATE CASCADE changes child keys when the parent key changes. This is only needed with natural keys; surrogate (ID) keys never change, which is one reason they are preferred.

**Expected result:** 2 rows

| BedID | WardCode |
|---|---|
| 1 | CARD |
| 2 | CARD |

**Question:** In one transaction, insert a good patient and then a duplicate. Use XACT_ABORT and TRY/CATCH so that neither row is saved, and report what happened.

```sql
SET XACT_ABORT ON;
BEGIN TRY
    BEGIN TRANSACTION;
        INSERT INTO healthcare.Patients VALUES (501, N'New Patient', 'PT-0000501', 'North');
        INSERT INTO healthcare.Patients VALUES (1,   N'Duplicate',   'PT-0000502', 'North');
    COMMIT;
END TRY
BEGIN CATCH
    IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0 ROLLBACK;
    SELECT ERROR_NUMBER() AS ErrorNo,
           (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM healthcare.Patients WHERE PatientID = 501) AS Patient501Saved;
END CATCH;
```

**How it works:** XACT_ABORT ON rolls back the whole transaction on an error, so the first insert is undone too (Patient501Saved = 0). This all-or-nothing behaviour is what "atomic" means in ACID.

**Expected result:** 1 row

| ErrorNo | Patient501Saved |
|---|---|
| 2627 | 0 |

**Question:** Create a table dbo.TempReport, then move it into the reporting schema.

```sql
CREATE SCHEMA reporting;
GO
CREATE TABLE dbo.TempReport (ID int);
ALTER SCHEMA reporting TRANSFER dbo.TempReport;

SELECT SCHEMA_NAME(schema_id) AS SchemaName, name FROM sys.tables WHERE name = 'TempReport';
```

**How it works:** ALTER SCHEMA ... TRANSFER moves an object between schemas. Permissions granted directly on the object are dropped.

**Expected result:** 1 row

| SchemaName | name |
|---|---|
| reporting | TempReport |

**Question:** Give report_reader SELECT on Patients only. Then, acting as report_reader, check which tables it can read.

```sql
CREATE USER report_reader WITHOUT LOGIN;
GRANT SELECT ON healthcare.Patients TO report_reader;

EXECUTE AS USER = 'report_reader';
SELECT t.name AS TableName,
       HAS_PERMS_BY_NAME('healthcare.' + t.name, 'OBJECT', 'SELECT') AS CanSelect
FROM sys.tables AS t
WHERE SCHEMA_NAME(t.schema_id) = 'healthcare';
REVERT;
```

**How it works:** EXECUTE AS lets you test as another user; REVERT switches back. Note that sys.tables only shows objects the user has some permission on, so the result lists just Patients. Metadata visibility is part of SQL Server's security model.

**Expected result:** 1 row

| TableName | CanSelect |
|---|---|
| Patients | 1 |

**Question:** Create a role clinical_reader, give it SELECT on Encounters and Prescriptions, and add report_reader to it.

```sql
CREATE ROLE clinical_reader;
GRANT SELECT ON healthcare.Encounters    TO clinical_reader;
GRANT SELECT ON healthcare.Prescriptions TO clinical_reader;

CREATE USER report_reader WITHOUT LOGIN;
ALTER ROLE clinical_reader ADD MEMBER report_reader;

SELECT r.name AS RoleName, m.name AS MemberName
FROM sys.database_role_members AS rm
JOIN sys.database_principals AS r ON r.principal_id = rm.role_principal_id
JOIN sys.database_principals AS m ON m.principal_id = rm.member_principal_id
WHERE r.name = 'clinical_reader';
```

**How it works:** Grant permissions to roles, then add users to roles. Managing roles is much easier than managing many individual users.

**Expected result:** 1 row

| RoleName | MemberName |
|---|---|
| clinical\_reader | report\_reader |

**Question:** Explain the difference between REVOKE and DENY, and show DENY overriding a role grant.

```sql
CREATE ROLE clinical_reader;
GRANT SELECT ON healthcare.Patients TO clinical_reader;
CREATE USER report_reader WITHOUT LOGIN;
ALTER ROLE clinical_reader ADD MEMBER report_reader;
DENY SELECT ON healthcare.Patients TO report_reader;

EXECUTE AS USER = 'report_reader';
SELECT HAS_PERMS_BY_NAME('healthcare.Patients', 'OBJECT', 'SELECT') AS CanSelectPatients;
REVERT;
```

**How it works:** REVOKE removes a previous GRANT or DENY (back to neutral). DENY explicitly blocks, and it wins over any GRANT, even one that comes through a role.

**Expected result:** 1 row

| CanSelectPatients |
|---|
| 0 |

**Question:** Allow report_reader to read only PatientID and Region from Patients (not names).

```sql
CREATE USER report_reader WITHOUT LOGIN;
GRANT SELECT ON healthcare.Patients (PatientID, Region) TO report_reader;

EXECUTE AS USER = 'report_reader';
SELECT Region, COUNT(*) AS Patients FROM healthcare.Patients GROUP BY Region;
REVERT;
```

**How it works:** Column-level GRANT limits access to sensitive columns such as names. Selecting PatientName as report_reader would fail.

