| North | 24 |
| South | 36 |
| West | 24 |

**Question:** Create a view that hides patient names, and give report_reader access to the view only.

```sql
CREATE VIEW healthcare.vPatientRegions AS
SELECT PatientID, Region FROM healthcare.Patients;
GO
CREATE USER report_reader WITHOUT LOGIN;
GRANT SELECT ON healthcare.vPatientRegions TO report_reader;

EXECUTE AS USER = 'report_reader';
SELECT COUNT(*) AS VisibleRows FROM healthcare.vPatientRegions;
REVERT;
```

**How it works:** Ownership chaining: the view and table have the same owner, so the user only needs permission on the view. Views are a classic way to expose part of a table.

**Expected result:** 1 row

| VisibleRows |
|---|
| 100 |

**Question:** report_reader is leaving the team. Remove them from the clinical_reader role and drop the user. Confirm they are gone.

```sql
CREATE ROLE clinical_reader;
CREATE USER report_reader WITHOUT LOGIN;
ALTER ROLE clinical_reader ADD MEMBER report_reader;

ALTER ROLE clinical_reader DROP MEMBER report_reader;
DROP USER report_reader;

SELECT COUNT(*) AS UserStillExists
FROM sys.database_principals WHERE name = 'report_reader';
```

**How it works:** Remove role memberships, then drop the user. A user who owns a schema or objects cannot be dropped until ownership is moved (ALTER AUTHORIZATION).

**Expected result:** 1 row

| UserStillExists |
|---|
| 0 |

**Question:** Make report_reader see only North-region patients using row-level security.

```sql
CREATE SCHEMA rls;
GO
CREATE FUNCTION rls.fn_RegionFilter (@Region varchar(10))
RETURNS TABLE WITH SCHEMABINDING AS
RETURN SELECT 1 AS ok
       WHERE USER_NAME() <> 'report_reader' OR @Region = 'North';
GO
CREATE SECURITY POLICY rls.PatientRegionPolicy
ADD FILTER PREDICATE rls.fn_RegionFilter(Region) ON healthcare.Patients
WITH (STATE = ON);
GO
CREATE USER report_reader WITHOUT LOGIN;
GRANT SELECT ON healthcare.Patients TO report_reader;

EXECUTE AS USER = 'report_reader';
SELECT Region, COUNT(*) AS VisiblePatients FROM healthcare.Patients GROUP BY Region;
REVERT;
```

**How it works:** A security policy adds the filter function to every query on the table automatically, so report_reader sees 24 North patients while others see all 100. Healthcare systems use this to limit staff to their own clinic.

**Expected result:** 1 row

| Region | VisiblePatients |
|---|---|
| North | 24 |

**Question:** Mask PatientNumber so non-privileged users see only the last 3 characters.

```sql
ALTER TABLE healthcare.Patients
ALTER COLUMN PatientNumber ADD MASKED WITH (FUNCTION = 'partial(0,"XXXXXXX",3)');

CREATE USER report_reader WITHOUT LOGIN;
GRANT SELECT ON healthcare.Patients TO report_reader;

EXECUTE AS USER = 'report_reader';
SELECT TOP (3) PatientID, PatientNumber FROM healthcare.Patients ORDER BY PatientID;
REVERT;
```

**How it works:** Dynamic data masking changes what is displayed, not what is stored. Users with the UNMASK permission see real values. It is not encryption, so combine it with permissions.

**Expected result:** 3 rows

| PatientID | PatientNumber |
|---|---|
| 1 | XXXXXXX001 |
| 2 | XXXXXXX002 |
| 3 | XXXXXXX003 |

